us founders sort of sat  down and thought about what it was  that we wanted to do with the money and  i think all five of us being  first-generation physicians  and having looked back on our road and  trajectories  into medicine really thought that  starting this foundation would be a  great way to give back to other young  students like us  um which we do in the form of  scholarships i love that  and it just also shows like if you build  it they will come if you  put a logo on it it's real and this is  just such a great way to incentivize  people to just start  um dr greene why do you think that there  is such a lack of black women and male  doctors  i think it's a great question and it's  my belief is is that it's really only  due to a lack of  opportunity and a lack of support or  encouragement  and it's not just my belief there's data  to support that  we know that black students  young students like school-age children  have they're more likely to be at  schools with limited resources  they are less likely to be enrolled in  stem courses or  summer enrichment programs that pipeline  as we call it in  in medicine and medical education the  pipeline to  medical education is is not a very  um continuous one when it comes to  minority students  that can be because of lack of  opportunity and lack of  access but also i think what's hugely  important  is the lack of support and lack of  encouragement for  that little black girl or little black  boy who might  might not see a black physician for  their entire upbringing um and then  when they go to the doctor as a young  adult they might not have a black  physician and  who can they turn to for encouragement  or who can they turn to to say  how do i get in your shoes if they don't  see it and so for  us for white coats black doctors the the  phrase that i like to say is that our  visibility  is our activism and so being out in  public  wearing our t-shirts and our polos and  uh  selling sweatshirts and all those thing